<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gurs internment camp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gurs was an internment camp in the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, built in 1939 for refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime it held foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and others, including some 6,500 Jews deported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and the Saar-Palatinate in October 1940. From 1942 many of its inmates were sent via Drancy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gurs-internment-camp.jpg" alt="Gurs internment camp" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logor_Gurs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The camp stood at Gurs, not far from Pau. It was about 1.4 km long and 200 m wide, with a single street along its length and thirteen fenced blocks, called îlots, on either side, holding 382 cabins in all. The cabins, a type designed for the French army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, were thin planks covered with tarred fabric, without windows or insulation, and up to 60 people slept on sacks of straw in each 25 square metres. Heavy rain kept the clay ground permanently muddy, and there was no running water or plumbing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From April 1939 the camp received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>, ground crews of the Republican air force and members of the International Brigades, and that July some 17,000 internees of Spanish origin were held there. Most of the Spanish farmers were encouraged to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where they were handed to the Francoist authorities at Irún. From May 1940 the French government also interned German nationals, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> who had fled the Nazi regime, along with left-wing activists, pacifists, sympathisers of the Nazis and ordinary prisoners, known together as the undesirables.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> the camp held foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, political dissidents, stateless persons and others. In October 1940, in Operation Wagner-Bürckel, the Gauleiter Josef Bürckel had the Jews of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> sent there, the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of Jews westward by the Nazi regime, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> and dysentery were rife. Aid organisations, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>, the YMCA and the Oeuvre de Secours aux Enfants, which took many children out, worked in the camp, and 755 internees escaped. After the SS captain Theodor Dannecker inspected Gurs on 18 July 1942, its Jews were sent in convoys to Drancy from 6 August, most of them on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>.</p><p>After the liberation the camp briefly held German prisoners of war, accused collaborators and Spaniards who had fought in the French Resistance, whom the French feared might take up arms against Franco on the border. It was dismantled in 1946; some 64,000 people had been held there over the years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A reconstructed cabin and several monuments stand on the site, and a commemorative ceremony is held each year. The Jewish cemetery is maintained at the expense of the German cities that deported their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to Gurs, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, Freiburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, and since 1985 a memorial has honoured the fighters of the Spanish Civil War interned there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gurs internment camp” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nazis took control in March 1933 under Reich governor Robert Wagner, who in 1940 deported Baden's Jews to the <b>Gurs</b> camp in France.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-baden">Republic of Baden</a></li></ul></section>
